    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d181">

                <pb facs="0387" n="313" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg313"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The President to the Chief of
                        Staff, United States Army</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Marshall</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-01-09">January 9,
                        1942</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Memorandum for General
                        Marshall</hi></p>
                <p>In view of the rather tense situation caused by the announcement of Wavell’s
                    appointment, without prior approval by the Dutch Government in London, I am
                    wondering if it would not relieve the tension a bit if you could—this
                    afternoon—go over the list of the Dutch request for munitions of war and make
                    some releases to them, even though they be very modest, such as some light
                    tanks, depth charges and sub-machine guns.</p>
                <p>I think it is very important in our own relations with the Dutch that they fully
                    understand that we are going to do everything we possibly can for them.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>F. D. R.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
